  <info><![CDATA[# ODD for Enhanced Evolution of Sex Model

Rhett Adam, EES 4760 Individual Research Project

## Purpose and patterns

This ODD describes an enhancement of Kristin Crouse’s Evolution of Sex model (CoMSES,
version 1.2.1), in which coevolving parasites can keep sexual reproduction alive against asexual competitors. The enhanced model asks which reproductive strategy wins under which local conditions, and how sharply the winners are separated in space. It adds self-fertilization as a third strategy and environmental gradients, so strategies compete locally instead of in one mixed population.

Four questions guide this study. Do self-fertilizers beat outcrossers when parasites cannot evolve and fail when parasites coevolve, as in experimental work on self-fertilizing hosts (Morran et al. 2011)? When parasite pressure varies across space, does a stable boundary form between strategies, and what sets its position and sharpness? Does a second gradient that makes mate finding harder let self-fertilizing take habitat from outcrossing? Does parasite evolution shift the boundary?

With all new modules off, the model must reproduce the behavior of the original. Asexual snails should replace sexual ones without parasites, and sexual reproduction should persist when infection is frequent, and mates are easy to find. I will judge the new modules by whether selffertilization heterozygosity falls quickly, whether parasite strategies track the most common host phenotypes within each region, and whether strategy shares change consistently along a gradient.

## Entities, state variables, and scales

The model has two types of agents, snails and parasites, plus spatial units (patches) and an environment.

**Snails** are diploid. Their state variables are allele-color-1 and allele-color-2 (orange or blue, with blue dominant), allele-size-1 and allele-size-2 (1 or 3, codominant, so size is their mean), sex, infecting-parasite (the parasite infecting the snail, or nobody), position (x and y in patch units), and heading (degrees). Sex sets the reproductive strategy and is male, female, asexual, or self-fertile. It is fixed for life and inherited, except that offspring of sexual parents are male or female with equal probability. Color and size together give six phenotypes.

**Parasites** are haploid. Their state variables are color and size, which define the infection strategy and take one of the same six phenotypes, my-host (the snail it infects, or nobody), position, and heading. A parasite can infect only a snail with the same color and size.

**Patches** have a fixed location (column and row) and three display trails: asexual-trail, sexual-trail, and self-fertilizing-trail. Each is a unitless value from 0 to 1 that marks recent presence of that strategy.

**The environment** consists of two fixed gradients, salinity from 0 in the west to 1 in the east and temperature from 0 in the south to 1 in the north. Salinity, temperature, and the local infection chance and mate radius derived from them are calculated from patch position and the gradient parameters, so they are not state variables.

**Scales.** One patch is one arbitrary distance unit, and one tick is one arbitrary time step. The world is 300 by 300 patches, and runs last 5,000 ticks for baseline checks and 10,000 ticks for gradient runs. Snails move 0.3 patches per tick, and parasites move 0.7 patches per tick. When a gradient is on, the world does not wrap along that axis, and agents turn around at the edge, so the extremes of the gradient are never adjacent.

## Process overview and scheduling
The model runs in discrete time steps (ticks), with each tick consisting of a sequence of
environmental, snail, and parasite processes. At the beginning of each tick, local population density is calculated. Snails then move and reproduce in randomized order, after which population size is regulated through density-dependent culling.

When infection is possible, local crowding is recalculated, and parasites undergo their
movement, infection, reproduction, and mortality processes. Free parasites move independently and attempt to infect nearby snails, while parasites already infecting a host move with that host and reproduce. Parasite population size is then regulated by enforcing population bounds. Finally, strategy-presence trails are updated, and model outputs are recorded at the specified sampling interval.

All agents within a population are activated in randomized order, and changes to the model occur immediately rather than being held until the end of a tick. Consequently, agents acting later in a tick may respond to changes caused by agents that acted earlier. Newly produced offspring enter the population immediately but do not become eligible to act until the following tick.

The scheduling sequence is summarized below:

Environmental and population calculations: Calculate local population density.

Snail processes: Randomized movement, reproduction, and population regulation.

Parasite processes: When infection is possible, calculate crowding, move and infect, reproduce,and regulate parasite mortality and population size.

Observation: Update strategy-presence traits and record model outputs at the sampling interval.

## Design concepts

**Basic principles.** The model rests on the Red Queen hypothesis, in which parasites favor common host genotypes and so reward hosts that reshuffle their genes. It adds two ideas: that mating systems trade the costs of males and mate finding against the benefits of recombination, and that parasite pressure can differ from place to place. The model tests whether these principles still hold when strategies compete locally.

**Emergence.** Strategy frequencies, host and parasite phenotype cycles, extinctions, and the position and sharpness of strategy boundaries emerge from reproduction, infection, and
movement.

**Adaptation.** Agents have no adaptive behavior. Populations adapt through differential
survival and reproduction of genotypes and parasite strategies, with mutation and recombination supplying variation.

**Objectives.** Not used.

**Learning.** Not used.

**Prediction.** Not used.

**Sensing.** Females sense males within their local mate radius, and free parasites sense
uninfected snails on their own patch. Each agent reads the gradient values of its own patch only.

**Interaction.** Mating and infection are direct interactions. Snails compete indirectly through carrying capacity and parasites through crowding, so strategies interact only through these limits and through the parasites.

**Stochasticity.** Movement, reproduction, inheritance, mutation, mate choice, infection,
parasite death, culling, and all initial conditions are random, which is why results are
summarized over replicates.

**Collectives.** Not used.

**Observation.** The original plots of strategy share and phenotype counts are kept. New
outputs record strategy share and heterozygosity through time and strategy share along the
gradient, as described under record-output.

## Initialization
At time 0, snail-carrying-capacity snails (5,000 by default) are placed at uniformly random positions with random headings. A fraction initial-percent-sexual of them are sexual, each male or female with equal probability; a fraction initial-percent-self-fertilizing are self-fertilizers, and the rest are asexual. Strategy is assigned independently of position, so every strategy starts spread across the whole world, and any spatial pattern comes from selection. Each color allele is orange or blue, and each size allele is 1 or 3, with equal probability. If any infection chance is
above 0, 10% of snail-carrying-capacity parasites (500 by default) are placed freely at random positions, each with one of the six strategies with equal probability. All trials start at 0. Initial values are arbitrary and not based on data, and they are the same in every run apart from random draws. Replicates use different random seeds, and the same seed list is reused across treatments.

## Input data
The model does not use input data to represent time-varying processes. Salinity and temperature are generated by a formula at setup and stay constant during a run.

## Submodels

**Environmental fields.** With x the patch column, W the world width, xc the gradient center, and w the gradient width as a fraction of W, salinity is a ramp.

S(x) = min (1, max (0, 0.5 + (x – xc) / (wW)))

The local infection chance is I(x) = Iw + (Ie - Iw)S(x). Temperature T(y) is linear in the patch row, and a female’s search radius is r(y) = r0(1 – c (1 – T(y))), with r0 the base mating radius and c the cold-mating penalty.

**Reproduction.** An uninfected snail that passes the fertility draw reproduces by strategy. Asexual snails clone. A female picks a random male within her local radius and fails if none is in range. A self-fertile snail hatches one offspring from two of its own gametes, and its fertility is multiplied by one minus self-fertilizing-cost. In the original code, a sexual offspring takes at each locus either its mother’s or its father’s allele from the same slot, which, applied to one parent, returns an exact clone. When mendelian-segregation? is on, each parent instead contributes one gamete carrying a randomly chosen one of its two alleles per locus, so a self-fertilizing heterozygote gives the usual 1 to 2 to 1 genotype ratio. Offspring inherit the parent’s strategy and mutate as in the original.

**Infection.** A free parasite picks an uninfected snail on its patch, and infection needs an exact color and size match and a draw below the local infection chance.

**Parasite release and evolution.** In the original code, a new parasite copies its parent’s host and leaves only if it mutates, so the mutation rate also controls dispersal. With release-offspring? on, every new parasite is free. Parasite evolution is then varied through the mutation rate. At 0, no new strategies appear; at 0.1, parasites coevolve, and at 1.0, strategies are random and cannot track hosts.

**Observation.** Every 25 ticks, the model records counts of each strategy and parasite type, mean heterozygosity by strategy, and strategy counts in 20 vertical strips. After averaging the last 500 ticks, boundary position is where a strategy’s share crosses 50%, and width is the distance between its 25% and 75% crossings.

## Planned experiments
Experiment 0 checks that the new switches leave the baseline unchanged, with and without
mendelian-segregation?. E1 starts all three strategies at one-third each in a uniform world, varying infection chance and self-fertilizing cost. E2 varies parasite mutation rate over 0, 0.1, and 1.0 to test whether self-fertilizing wins against a parasite that cannot track it and fails against one that can. E3 turns on the salinity gradient, varying gradient width and mating radius, first with sexual against asexual snails and then with all three strategies. E4 varies the infection range and mutation rate to test whether the boundary tracks the local infection chance at which sex persists in a uniform world. E5, if time allows, adds the temperature gradient and maps which strategy dominates each region. Each condition uses at least 15 replicates, and results report medians and replicate outcome counts.

## References
Crouse, K. (2026). Evolution of Sex (Version 1.2.1). CoMSES Computational Model Library.
https://www.comses.net/codebases/5051/releases/1.2.1/

Morran, L. T., Schmidt, O. G., Gelarden, I. A., Parrish, R. C., II, & Lively, C. M. (2011). Running with the Red Queen: Host-parasite coevolution selects for biparental sex. *Science, 333(6039)*, 216–218. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1206360

## Hybrid defaults and interactive tuning protocol

Interface switches stay **off** so Setup matches the original baseline. New numeric defaults are pre-tuned so flipping a module on shows a clear effect without hunting for values first.

**patch-display chooser** paints the world so every env knob is visible:
- `none` — white background (baseline look)
- `strategies` — yellow asexual / violet sexual / lime selfing trails
- `salinity` — S(x) ramp (scrub `gradient-center` / `gradient-width`)
- `infection` — infection field (uniform `parasite-infection-chance`, or west→east when salinity is on)
- `temperature` — T(y) south→north
- `mate-radius` — relative female search factor under `cold-mating-penalty`

**What “good” looks like (from your baseline analysis + ODD):**
1. Modules off, infection 0 → asexual wins; infection ~70%, mating radius 50% → sexual usually persists.
2. Mendelian on + selfing present → `het self` / Heterozygosity plot falls toward 0 while sexual het stays higher.
3. `release-offspring?` on → `# free` parasites rises; mutation 0 / 0.1 / 1.0 is the Morran-style contrast (selfing wins when parasites cannot track).
4. Salinity on, west≈0 east≈70, patch-display=`infection` then `strategies` → asexual west, sexual east; boundary monitors move with the ramp.
5. Temperature on + cold penalty → sexuals struggle in the cold (south); selfing can take that habitat.

**Suggested interactive tuning order (you drive the knobs):**
1. Confirm baseline (all switches off) still gives the Red Queen result at infection 70%.
2. Turn on `mendelian-segregation?` and raise `initial-percent-self-fertilizing`; watch Heterozygosity.
3. Turn on `release-offspring?`; compare mutation 0 vs 0.1 vs 1.0.
4. Set patch-display to `infection`, turn on salinity, scrub west/east/width/center until the field looks right, then switch to `strategies` and run.
5. Add temperature + cold penalty last; use `mate-radius` display to verify the south shrinks search.

BehaviorSpace E0–E5 remain the formal sweep; use this Interface path to choose the values those experiments should center on.
]]></info>
